## Queue — async backpressured bounded queue (spec §4.4). The only channel
## in-tree before this is the SYNCHRONOUS MPSC (`Sender`/`Receiver`,
## std.sync) whose `recv` BLOCKS THE OS THREAD — fatal in rondo's
## thread-per-core model (blocking the thread stalls the whole per-core
## reactor). This is the async-aware alternative: a bounded ring buffer whose
## `send`/`recv` PARK THE TASK via the reactor, never the OS thread.
##
## v1 surface (what the current lowering supports):
##   * `Queue.bounded(capacity, overflow)` → a split `(AsyncSender,
##     AsyncReceiver)` pair sharing one refcounted C control block. Mirrors
##     the existing `Sender`/`Receiver` split so the borrow checker tracks
##     producer and consumer separately. Capacity is MANDATORY — there is NO
##     unbounded constructor (Go's deliberate fixed-capacity discipline).
##   * `enum Overflow { Suspend; DropOldest; DropNewest }` — the ZIO overflow
##     taxonomy: backpressure / sliding / dropping. VARIANT ORDER is the
##     load-bearing contract: tags must match `RUXEN_OVERFLOW_*` in
##     `runtime/queue.c` (Suspend=0, DropOldest=1, DropNewest=2). A no-payload
##     enum crosses to C as a HEAP POINTER whose int32 tag at offset 0 the C
##     reads (`*(int32_t *)overflow`) — NOT a bare i64 tag (the `Shutdown` enum
##     ABI in std.net; an unknown/null tag panics rather than defaulting).
##   * `tx.send(v)` → `SendFuture`; `rx.recv` → `RecvFuture`. HAND-WRITTEN
##     futures (async methods on classes aren't lowered), shaped like
##     `AsyncReadWithTimeoutFuture`: `queue_ptr`/`done` + `include Future` +
##     `def var poll` + `def drop`. On would-block, `poll` records the parked
##     task's WAKER on the queue's wake slot and returns `Poll.Pending`; the
##     peer's enqueue/dequeue wakes that task via the reactor. The TASK parks;
##     the OS thread never blocks. Drive them with `.await` (Shape-3 instance-
##     method awaitee) inside a top-level `async def`, or hand-poll.
##   * Element/error channel is `Int` (the established Task/Race/Scope
##     convention; typed-generic `Queue[T]` recovery deferred with the
##     multi-threaded round). `send`/`recv` resolve `Result[Int, QueueError]`.
##
## LEAK-FREE CLOSE BY OWNERSHIP (the ZIO/Go-leak fix):
##   * dropping the LAST `AsyncSender` marks the queue send-closed, so a parked
##     `recv` resolves `Err(QueueClosed)` (closes Go's abandoned-receiver leak:
##     a recv waiting on a queue whose producers are all gone is woken + told
##     the channel is done);
##   * dropping the `AsyncReceiver` marks the queue recv-closed, so a parked
##     `Suspend`-`send` resolves `Err(QueueClosed)` (closes Go's forgotten-
##     sender leak). The C control block is refcounted by BOTH ends and freed
##     only when the last end drops.
##
## v1 LIMITATION — SINGLE PARKED WAITER PER DIRECTION. The control block has
## ONE recv wake slot and ONE send wake slot, so v1 supports at most one
## IN-FLIGHT recv (single-consumer) and one in-flight PARKED send under
## `Overflow.Suspend`. Multiple producers (via `share`) work as long as they do
## not BLOCK on a full queue simultaneously; a waiter LIST for multi-producer
## backpressure is deferred to v2 (Codex high finding, documented).
##
## v1 LIMITATION — SINGLE-REACTOR (spec §4.4 risk 3 / §6, the largest genuine
## unknown): producer and consumer live on ONE worker/reactor. A cross-core
## wake (an AsyncSender on core A waking an AsyncReceiver on core B) needs a
## wake routed to a SPECIFIC remote reactor handle, which the single-thread/
## per-reactor executor does not evidence a primitive for. DEFERRED to v2.
## Because of single-reactor, no locking is needed and the refcounts are plain
## (not atomic) — the "SharedSync-style ATOMIC refcount" lands with the
## multi-threaded scheduler round.
##
## OWNERSHIP CONTRACT: an `AsyncSender`/`AsyncReceiver` value owns its share of
## the control block; `def drop` releases it. `Queue.bounded` returns the two
## ends by value (`Queue.bounded` returns a `QueueEnds` handle; pull `sender` /
## `receiver` off it) — move each into the side that uses it (e.g. into a
## spawned producer/consumer `async def`). Use `AsyncSender.share` for multiple
## producers.

## The overflow policy when a `send` meets a full buffer. ORDER PINNED to the
## C `RUXEN_OVERFLOW_*` tags — append-only, never reorder (a no-payload enum
## crosses to C as a heap pointer whose int32 tag at offset 0 `ruxen_queue_new`
## reads).
enum Overflow
  Suspend     # backpressure: the producer parks until a slot frees
  DropOldest  # sliding:   evict the head element, enqueue the new one
  DropNewest  # dropping:  reject the incoming element, keep the buffer
end

## The error arm of a `send`/`recv` result: the queue's other end is gone.
## A data-less marker (presence in `Err` is the whole signal), like the
## channel `SendError`/`RecvError` markers.
enum QueueError
  QueueClosed
end

## The future returned by `rx.recv`. Holds the shared control-block pointer
## (NOT owned — the AsyncReceiver owns the end-refcount; this op future only
## borrows the pointer to poll) + a `done` latch. `poll` drives the C
## `recv_step`: READY → dequeue a value (Ok); CLOSED → send-closed + drained
## (Err(QueueClosed)); BLOCK → park (waker recorded C-side), Pending.
class RecvFuture
  queue_ptr: Int
  waker: Int
  done: Int
  include Future
  type Output = Result[Int, QueueError]

  def init(queue_ptr: Int)
    self.queue_ptr = queue_ptr
    self.waker = 0
    self.done = 0
  end

  def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Result[Int, QueueError]]
    if self.done != 0
      return Poll.Pending
    end
    # `cx: &var Context` flows into the step as its raw RuxenContext* at the
    # i64 ABI; the step reads cx's waker to know which task to wake on a later
    # enqueue/close. Returns: 0 = would-block (parked), 1 = value ready,
    # 2 = closed.
    let code = ruxen_queue_recv_step(self.queue_ptr, cx)
    if code == 0
      # Parked: remember WHICH waker we registered so `def drop` can vacate
      # exactly that slot if this future is cancelled/dropped while parked —
      # otherwise the slot would dangle into a freed Context (UAF on the next
      # peer wake). Codex blocker fix.
      self.waker = ruxen_queue_recv_waker(self.queue_ptr)
      return Poll.Pending
    end
    # Resolved (value or close): we are no longer parked, so forget any waker.
    self.waker = 0
    self.done = 1
    if code == 2
      return Poll.Ready(Result.Err(QueueError.QueueClosed))
    end
    let value = ruxen_queue_recv_taken_value(self.queue_ptr)
    Poll.Ready(Result.Ok(value))
  end

  def drop
    # If this future was DROPPED/CANCELLED while parked, vacate its wake slot
    # so a later peer enqueue/close does not wake a dangling waker (the cancel
    # fn clears the slot ONLY if it still holds OUR waker). The control block
    # itself is owned by the AsyncReceiver end, not this op future — we only
    # release our hold on the slot.
    if self.queue_ptr != 0 && self.waker != 0
      ruxen_queue_cancel_recv(self.queue_ptr, self.waker)
    end
    self.waker = 0
    self.queue_ptr = 0
  end
end

## The future returned by `tx.send(v)`. Holds the control-block pointer + the
## value to enqueue + a `done` latch. `poll` drives the C `send_step`: READY →
## enqueued (Ok); DROPPED → the policy dropped it but the send still resolves
## (Ok); CLOSED → recv-closed (Err(QueueClosed)); BLOCK → full + Suspend, park.
class SendFuture
  queue_ptr: Int
  value: Int
  waker: Int
  done: Int
  include Future
  type Output = Result[Int, QueueError]

  def init(queue_ptr: Int, value: Int)
    self.queue_ptr = queue_ptr
    self.value = value
    self.waker = 0
    self.done = 0
  end

  def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Result[Int, QueueError]]
    if self.done != 0
      return Poll.Pending
    end
    # 0 = would-block (full + Suspend, parked), 1 = enqueued, 2 = closed,
    # 3 = dropped-by-policy (resolves Ok — non-blocking policy).
    let code = ruxen_queue_send_step(self.queue_ptr, cx, self.value)
    if code == 0
      # Parked on a full Suspend queue — remember our wake slot (cancel-while-
      # parked UAF guard, Codex blocker fix; see RecvFuture.poll).
      self.waker = ruxen_queue_send_waker(self.queue_ptr)
      return Poll.Pending
    end
    self.waker = 0
    self.done = 1
    if code == 2
      return Poll.Ready(Result.Err(QueueError.QueueClosed))
    end
    # code 1 (enqueued) or 3 (dropped-by-policy): the send completed. Return
    # the value the producer offered (0-payload Ok — the channel carries the
    # success, not a meaningful value).
    Poll.Ready(Result.Ok(self.value))
  end

  def drop
    if self.queue_ptr != 0 && self.waker != 0
      ruxen_queue_cancel_send(self.queue_ptr, self.waker)
    end
    self.waker = 0
    self.queue_ptr = 0
  end
end

## The producing end. Owns one sender-refcount of the shared control block;
## `def drop` releases it (the LAST sender drop closes the send side).
class AsyncSender
  include Send

  queue_ptr: Int

  def init(queue_ptr: Int)
    self.queue_ptr = queue_ptr
  end

  ## Send `value`. Returns a `SendFuture` you `.await` (Shape-3) or hand-poll.
  ## Under `Overflow.Suspend` a full queue parks the producer until the
  ## consumer drains a slot; under DropOldest/DropNewest it never parks.
  def send(value: Int) -> SendFuture
    SendFuture.new(self.queue_ptr, value)
  end

  ## Make an additional producer end (bumps the C sender refcount; both
  ## AsyncSender values share the control block). The send side closes only
  ## when the LAST sender drops. Named `share` (NOT `clone`): `clone` is the
  ## compiler-synthesised field-copy method — overriding it collides at
  ## codegen — and these semantics (refcount bump, shared backing) are a
  ## deliberate SHARE, not a value copy.
  def share -> AsyncSender
    AsyncSender.new(ruxen_queue_sender_clone(self.queue_ptr))
  end

  ## `def drop` is an FFI-ALIAS drop (`as "ruxen_async_sender_end_drop"`), NOT an
  ## `.rx` body — matching the sync `Sender`/`Receiver`/`File`/`Mutex` precedent.
  ## A bootstrap stdlib class's `.rx`-BODY `def drop` is NOT picked up by MIR
  ## drop-elaboration for a scope-exit local (verified: a local AsyncSender with
  ## an .rx-body drop never fired `ruxen_queue_sender_drop`), whereas the
  ## FFI-alias form IS elaborated (the established stdlib drop shape). The C
  ## helper receives THIS AsyncSender's heap pointer (`{ int64 queue_ptr }`),
  ## reads the control-block ptr from it, and runs the sender-close logic. The
  ## MIR pass emits `AsyncSender_drop(p) + ruxen_dealloc(p)` at scope exit, and
  ## the last sender's drop closes the send side.
  lib "runtime/queue.c"
    def drop as "ruxen_async_sender_end_drop" -> nil
  end
end

## The consuming end. Owns the receiver-refcount; `def drop` releases it
## (closing the recv side so a parked Suspend-send resolves Err(QueueClosed)).
## SINGLE-CONSUMER in v1 (one `recv` future in flight at a time).
class AsyncReceiver
  include Send

  queue_ptr: Int

  def init(queue_ptr: Int)
    self.queue_ptr = queue_ptr
  end

  ## Receive the next value. Returns a `RecvFuture` you `.await` or hand-poll.
  ## Parks the TASK (via the reactor) when the queue is empty; the next
  ## `send` wakes it. Resolves `Err(QueueClosed)` when the queue is drained
  ## AND every sender has dropped.
  def recv -> RecvFuture
    RecvFuture.new(self.queue_ptr)
  end

  ## FFI-alias drop (see AsyncSender.drop for why an .rx-body drop isn't
  ## elaborated for a bootstrap-class local). Receives this AsyncReceiver's heap
  ## pointer; reads the control-block ptr and runs the receiver-close logic.
  lib "runtime/queue.c"
    def drop as "ruxen_async_receiver_end_drop" -> nil
  end
end

## The two ends of a bounded queue, handed back by `Queue.bounded`. A thin
## handle over the shared control-block pointer with `sender`/`receiver`
## accessors that mint the typed ends.
##
## WHY a handle and not the spec sketch's `let (tx, rx) = Queue.bounded(...)`:
## tuple-destructuring a tuple whose ELEMENT TYPES are classes does not
## propagate those types to the bindings today — the call's return type stays
## an unresolved `Infer` and `tx.send` mangles as `?T_send` (verified this
## session; `(Int, Int)` tuples destructure fine, but class-typed tuple
## elements do not). That is a typeck inference gap (class-typed tuple return),
## NOT specific to Queue — deferred as a v1 follow-up. The handle uses only
## single-class static/instance returns (proven to resolve) and reads cleanly:
##   let ends = Queue.bounded(256, Overflow.Suspend)
##   let tx = ends.sender
##   let rx = ends.receiver
## The construction (`ruxen_queue_new`) starts the block with one sender ref +
## one receiver ref; `sender`/`receiver` HAND those founding refs out (they do
## NOT bump — the handle is a transient splitter, not a third owner). Call each
## accessor EXACTLY ONCE; use `tx.share` for additional producers.
class QueueEnds
  sender_ptr: Int    # founding sender ref; 0 once handed out
  receiver_ptr: Int  # founding receiver ref; 0 once handed out

  def init(queue_ptr: Int)
    self.sender_ptr = queue_ptr
    self.receiver_ptr = queue_ptr
  end

  ## Take the producing end (the founding sender ref). CONSUMING: each end is
  ## handed out EXACTLY ONCE. A second call panics rather than minting a second
  ## AsyncSender off the same founding ref (which would over-decrement
  ## sender_refs on drop → premature close + a dangling control block → UAF;
  ## Codex high finding). Use `tx.share` for additional producers.
  def var sender -> AsyncSender
    if self.sender_ptr == 0
      panic!("QueueEnds.sender already taken (use tx.share for more producers)")
    end
    let p = self.sender_ptr
    self.sender_ptr = 0
    AsyncSender.new(p)
  end

  ## Take the consuming end (the founding receiver ref). CONSUMING: call once
  ## (single-consumer v1); a second call panics.
  def var receiver -> AsyncReceiver
    if self.receiver_ptr == 0
      panic!("QueueEnds.receiver already taken (single-consumer v1)")
    end
    let p = self.receiver_ptr
    self.receiver_ptr = 0
    AsyncReceiver.new(p)
  end

  ## If an end was never taken, its founding ref must still be released so the
  ## control block is not leaked. A taken end (ptr 0) is skipped; the
  ## AsyncSender/AsyncReceiver that took it now owns that ref. Belt-and-braces:
  ## the common path takes both ends, so both ptrs are 0 here.
  def drop
    if self.sender_ptr != 0
      ruxen_queue_sender_drop(self.sender_ptr)
      self.sender_ptr = 0
    end
    if self.receiver_ptr != 0
      ruxen_queue_receiver_drop(self.receiver_ptr)
      self.receiver_ptr = 0
    end
  end
end

## Queue namespace — the constructor surface.
class Queue
  ## Construct a bounded queue with the given capacity (mandatory, >= 1) and
  ## overflow policy. Returns a `QueueEnds` handle; pull the typed `sender` /
  ## `receiver` ends off it. NO unbounded variant (Go's discipline).
  def self.bounded(capacity: Int, overflow: Overflow) -> QueueEnds
    QueueEnds.new(ruxen_queue_new(capacity, overflow))
  end
end

## §4.4 queue primitives (defined in runtime/queue.c). Module-level lib decls
## so the futures + ends call them by bare identifier. `cx: &var Context` flows
## into the step decls as the raw `RuxenContext*` at the i64 ABI (the `race.rx`
## precedent), which the step reads for the parked task's waker.
lib "runtime/queue.c"
  def ruxen_queue_new(capacity: Int, overflow: &Overflow) -> Int
  def ruxen_queue_recv_step(queue_ptr: Int, cx: &var Context) -> Int
  def ruxen_queue_recv_taken_value(queue_ptr: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_queue_recv_waker(queue_ptr: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_queue_send_step(queue_ptr: Int, cx: &var Context, value: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_queue_send_waker(queue_ptr: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_queue_cancel_recv(queue_ptr: Int, waker: Int) -> nil
  def ruxen_queue_cancel_send(queue_ptr: Int, waker: Int) -> nil
  def ruxen_queue_sender_drop(queue_ptr: Int) -> nil
  def ruxen_queue_sender_clone(queue_ptr: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_queue_receiver_drop(queue_ptr: Int) -> nil
end
